==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|bitterli}}, {{m|enm|biterli}}, {{m|enm|biterliche}}, from {{inh|en|ang|biterlīċe}}, {{m|ang|bitterlīċe|t=bitterly}}, equivalent to {{suffix|en|bitter|ly}}. Cognate with {{cog|nds-de|bitterlik|t=bitterly}}, {{cog|de|bitterlich|t=bitterly}}, {{cog|sv|bitterligen|t=bitterly}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|GA}} {{IPA|en|/ˈbɪtəɹli/}}
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|en|/ˈbɪtəli/}}
* {{hyph|en|bit|ter|ly}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-bitterly.ogg|Audio (US)}}

===Adverb===
{{en-adv}}

# In a [[bitter]] manner.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1898|author={{w|Winston Churchill (novelist)|Winston Churchill}}
|title={{w|The Celebrity}}|chapter=4
|passage=Judge Short had gone to town, and Farrar was off for a three days' cruise up the lake. I was '''bitterly''' regretting I had not gone with him when the distant notes of a coach horn reached my ear, and I descried a four-in-hand winding its way up the inn road from the direction of Mohair.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Johanna Spyri
|authorlink=Johanna Spyri
|by=Eileen Hall
|title=Heidi
|year=1956
|origdate=1880
|page=91
|passage=Heidi threw herself down beside Clara's chair and began to cry '''bitterly'''.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=October 1, 2011|author=Phil McNulty|work=BBC Sport
|title=[http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/eng_prem/15045708.stm Everton 0-2 Liverpool]
|passage=Liverpool's £58m strikeforce of Andy Carroll and Luis Suarez scored the goals that settled the Merseyside derby at Goodison Park - but Everton were left complaining '''bitterly''' about Jack Rodwell's controversial early red card.}}

====Usage notes====
Some adjectives commonly collocating with profusely: [[cold]], [[disappointed]], [[dividing]], [[frustrated]]

